WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.560
Robo is a receptor that keeps axons off the midline.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.940
So where is the repellent it receives?

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.653
In 1999, a run of papers in one issue of the journal Cell named it.

4
00:00:15.253 --> 00:00:18.753
It is Slit, a large protein secreted by the midline glia.

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:24.027
A companion paper showed that Slit proteins bind Robo receptors.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:29.917
Recombinant Slit2 repels embryonic spinal motor axons in culture.

7
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:35.257
The fly genetics tested the link with flies missing one copy of each gene.

8
00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:38.737
Flies missing one copy are called heterozygotes.

9
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.710
Here are made-up numbers in the spirit of that experiment.

10
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.270
Score 50 segments per genotype for wrong crossings.

11
00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:50.743
Missing one copy of slit, 2 percent of segments go wrong.

12
00:00:51.703 --> 00:00:53.863
Missing one copy of robo, 4

13
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:56.887
Now remove one copy of each gene.

14
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:03.107
Under a simple independent-error model, you would expect about 6 What would a much

15
00:01:03.107 --> 00:01:04.107
bigger number mean?

16
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.920
The made-up result is 32 That is far more than the singles together.

17
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:17.673
Use a toy version of the rule.

18
00:01:17.673 --> 00:01:21.833
Count only Robo1, and let each factor scale with gene dose.

19
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:25.213
Then one missing copy leaves half the push.

20
00:01:26.113 --> 00:01:27.953
One missing copy of each leaves a quarter.

21
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:32.027
Suppose errors simply tracked the push that was lost.

22
00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:35.067
Then the double would barely differ from the singles.

23
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.287
Now add a threshold.

24
00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:41.867
Segments vary, so the push varies a little from one segment to the next.

25
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.947
With half the push, almost every segment still sits above the threshold.

26
00:01:48.087 --> 00:01:49.087
Few fail.

27
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.520
With a quarter, the average push sits near the threshold.

28
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.960
Many segments drop below it.

29
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.830
A jump this large points to a genetic interaction.

30
00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.470
A shared pathway with a threshold explains it simply.

31
00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.630
These numbers alone cannot prove that model.

32
00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:11.713
The binding data and the null mutants say that the process is a ligand acting on a

33
00:02:11.713 --> 00:02:12.713
receptor.

34
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:14.297
One puzzle remains.

35
00:02:15.257 --> 00:02:18.677
If Slit is Robo's ligand, why do the null mutants look different?

36
00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.847
Flies have three Robo receptors.

37
00:02:27.587 --> 00:02:31.547
Losing Robo1 removes much of the repulsion, so axons cross freely.

38
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.890
Robo2 remains and still hears Slit.

39
00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.430
So axons are pushed out of the midline, though not kept from crossing back.

40
00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:45.333
Remove Robo1 and Robo2 together, and the ladder looks much like slit.

41
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.473
Losing Slit removes the push through every Robo.

42
00:02:50.473 --> 00:02:52.333
Axons at the midline are not pushed out.

43
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:57.713
The takeaway: the ligand is shared, and the receptors share the work.

44
00:02:58.593 --> 00:03:01.453
Near threshold, small losses can make large effects.
